In the aftermath of the Maibara eradication, the Shogunate enforced the policy of ''Sakoku'' (Closed Country) in 1672. However, unlike the total isolation practiced by some neighboring states, Okaiken adopted a sophisticated policy of "Selective Opening" designed to monopolize the benefits of foreign trade while filtering out dangerous ideologies. While missionaries and most Western merchants were expelled, the government designated specific "Gateway Ports" for authorized partners. The Batavians and Zong merchants were restricted to the man-made island of Tejima in [[Shinagawa Bay]], which became the primary conduit for Western science, medicine, and gunnery. Simultaneously, trade with [[Kwangju]] was maintained through the Tsushima Domain to preserve vital diplomatic channels with the Fosian mainland. Uniquely among Western powers, the Etruscans were granted limited docking rights in the southern port of Kagoshima in the sub-arctic Yagikoshu region for the trade of furs and timber, strictly provided they imported no religious texts.

This "semi-open" status allowed Okaiken to acquire a significant amount of Western knowledge under the ''Rangaku'' (Western Learning) system. Sanese scholars, through Batavian texts, kept abreast of the scientific and industrial revolutions occurring abroad, meaning the country was far less technologically backward than assumed when the Mercian "Iron Envoys" arrived in the mid-19th century. The policy continued until geopolitical pressures shifted in the early 1800s. [[Sukoyria]]n encroachments from the sub-arctic South led the Shogunate to extend direct military rule to the frontier territories of Yagikoshu, and the Ostrovs in 1815, signaling the beginning of the end for the isolationist system.

=== Saito Era (1870–1912) ===
{{Main|Saito Restoration|Constitution of the Empire of Mitoyagi|Lurandian Expansion}}

==== The Arrival of the Iron Envoys ====
[[File:Iron Envoys 1853.png|thumb|right|300px|Admiral Vicente Dasmariñas's "Iron Envoys" steam into Okami Bay on July 8, 1853, bringing working models of telegraphs, steam locomotives, and textile looms.]]

The policy of ''[[sakoku]]'' (isolation) had sealed Mitoyagi off from the world for nearly three centuries. While the Shogunate remained static, the outside world underwent the Industrial Revolution. In 1843, diplomatic overtures from the [[Dagit]] were rejected, but the isolationist dike finally burst in 1853. On July 8, 1853, a squadron of galleons, commanded by Admiral Vicente Dasmariñas, steamed into the bay of Okami. Dasmariñas's "Iron Envoys" brought a sophisticated threat: while his steam-powered frigates displayed superior weaponry, he also presented the Shogunate with working models of telegraphs, steam locomotives, and textile looms. The message was clear: modernize and trade, or be crushed by the sheer weight of progress.

[[File:Treaty of Amity 1858.png|thumb|left|250px|The Treaty of Amity and Commerce (1858) imposed "unequal" terms, stripping Okaiken of tariff autonomy and granting extraterritoriality to Dagiti and Zong citizens.]]

The following year, at the [[Convention of Iwaki]] on March 31, 1854, the Shogunate succumbed to the pressure. Dagit did not just demand open ports; they demanded "civilized relations." The resulting [[Treaty of Amity and Commerce (1858)|Treaty of Amity and Commerce]] (1858) imposed "unequal" terms, stripping Okaiken of the right to set its own tariffs and granting "extraterritoriality" to Dagiti and Zong citizens, effectively placing them above Sanese law. The signing of the treaties shattered the legitimacy of the [[Honda Shogunate]]. The samurai class, enraged by the influx of cheap Zong goods and the presence of "untouchable" foreigners in Okami, rallied around the slogan ''[[Sonnō Jōi]]'' ("Revere the Emperor, Expel the Barbarians").

==== The Fall of the Dual Sovereignty ====
[[File:Honda Yoshinobu 1870.png|thumb|right|250px|Honda Yoshinobu, the 16th and final Shogun, formally resigned in November 1870 in a bid to peacefully transfer power to the Imperial court.]]

The 16th Shogun, [[Honda Yoshinobu]], was a pragmatist. Recognizing that the feudal "Dual Sovereignty" created by his ancestor Honda Shigetada was too fractured to confront the industrialized West, he made a historic decision. On November 19, 1870, Yoshinobu formally "put his prerogatives at the Emperor's disposal," resigning his position as Shogun ten days later. This act, known as the ''[[Taisei Hōkan]]'', was intended to be a peaceful transfer of power, merging the military strength of the Honda clan with the spiritual authority of the Saito Emperor.

However, the transition was not bloodless. Hardline factions within the Shogunate—specifically the Yamanaka Branch retainers and the northern domains—refused to accept the abolition of the samurai order. In January 1871, these tensions erupted into the [[Boshin War]] (War of the Year of the Dragon). The war began with the [[Battle of Toba-Fushimi]], where the modernized Imperial forces—funded by merchant guilds and armed with modern rifles—decisively defeated the traditionalist samurai armies. The victory allowed the young Emperor Saito to officially accept Yoshinobu's resignation and strip the Honda clan of its feudal domains, though Yoshinobu himself was pardoned and integrated into the new peerage.

On January 3, 1871, Emperor Saito issued the [[Edict of Restoration]], formally ending the 260-year rule of the Honda Shogunate and declaring the birth of the Empire of Mitoyagi. The Emperor announced that "We shall henceforward exercise supreme authority in all the internal and external affairs of the country," signaling a new era of centralized imperial governance.

[[File:Emperor Saito Formal Portrait.png|thumb|left|250px|Official portrait of Emperor Saito, 1888.]]

The Saito Era (1870–1912) represents the genesis of the modern Sanese state. Following the Edict of Restoration, the archipelago underwent a metamorphosis that observers described as "living through centuries in decades." Under the enlightened but authoritarian rule of Emperor Saito, Okaiken dismantled the feudal apparatus of the Honda Shogunate and constructed a new imperial identity. This period is defined by the national slogan ''[[Fukoku Kyōhei]]'' ("Enrich the Country, Strengthen the Military") and the strategic synthesis of Western technology with Sanese spirit (''[[Wakon Yōsai]]'').

==== Dismantling the Old Order (1871–1880) ====
[[File:Charter Oath 1871.png|thumb|right|300px|The Five Articles of the Charter Oath, delivered at the Okami Imperial Palace on April 6, 1871, established the ideological foundations of the restoration.]]

The first decade of the empire was dominated by the "Great Liquidation" of feudal privilege. The new government issued the [[Charter Oath|Five Articles of the Charter Oath]] on April 6, 1871, calling for deliberative assemblies, unity across all classes, and the pursuit of knowledge throughout the world. The first Prime Minister, [[Juichi Miyazaki]] (1872–1875), a pragmatic bureaucrat from the former anti-Honda coalition, recognized that the decentralized ''han'' (domain) system was incompatible with a modern nation-state. In 1871, he orchestrated the [[Abolition of the Han System]] (''Haihan-chiken''). In a masterstroke of diplomacy, the ''daimyō'' were persuaded to voluntarily surrender their lands to the Emperor in exchange for generous government bonds and relief from their domain's debts. The 260 feudal domains were reorganized into 72 centralized prefectures governed by appointees from Okami.

The most dangerous challenge was the Disposition of the Samurai. The class numbered nearly 400,000 households and represented a monopoly on military force. Miyazaki's government dismantled this caste not through extermination, but through absorption. The Conscription Ordinance of 1873 declared that "all subjects are soldiers," theoretically stripping the samurai of their exclusive right to bear arms. However, rather than discarding them, the state channeled the samurai into the police force, the bureaucracy, and the officer corps of the new Imperial Army. The government commuted their hereditary rice stipends into lump-sum bonds in 1876, forcing the warrior class to enter the commercial economy. While this triggered minor rebellions in the west, culminating in the [[Satsuma Rebellion]] (1877), the integration was largely successful, infusing the modern state with the disciplined ethos of the former aristocracy.

==== The Constitutional Pivot (1881–1890) ====
[[File:Meiji Constitution 1889.png|thumb|left|300px|The promulgation of the Meiji Constitution on February 11, 1889, established a constitutional monarchy with a bicameral Diet.]]

As the feudal order faded, a fierce debate erupted over the shape of the new state. The "Freedom and People's Rights Movement" demanded a Western-style liberal democracy, while the ruling oligarchs favored a Prussian-style authoritarian monarchy. The debate came to a head under Prime Minister [[Enoki Kanezane]] (1881–1889). A conservative pragmatist, Enoki viewed unchecked democracy as a recipe for chaos. He dispatched commissions to study the constitutions of the Great Powers, ultimately rejecting the French and American models as too liberal.

The resulting [[Constitution of the Empire of Mitoyagi|Meiji Constitution]], promulgated on February 11, 1889, was a deliberate hybrid. It established the [[Diet of Mitoyagi|Imperial Diet]] with an elected [[House of Commoners (Okaiken)|House of Commoners]], satisfying the demand for representation. However, it placed sovereignty solely in the Emperor, declaring him "sacred and inviolable." Crucially, the military general staff was made independent of the Diet and the Prime Minister, answering only to the Emperor—a "Supreme Command" prerogative that would have disastrous consequences in the 1930s. The friction of this new system was immediate. The first Diet session in 1890 saw a collision between the elected "popular parties" and the "transcendent" cabinet of Prime Minister [[Mitsunari Taku]], who believed the government should be above political factionalism.

==== Industrialization and the Zaibatsu ====
[[File:First Railway 1876.png|thumb|right|250px|The opening of the first rail line connecting Okami to Tokuyama in 1876 symbolized the nation's rapid modernization.]]

The Saito government understood that military power depended on industrial capacity. Because the private sector was underdeveloped, the state took the lead, building "model factories" for silk reeling, cotton spinning, and shipbuilding. Once these industries became viable, they were sold at rock-bottom prices to politically connected merchant families—the Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Sumitomo, and Yasuda—birthing the ''[[Zaibatsu]]'' (financial cliques). This state-guided capitalism transformed the landscape. The first railway connected Okami and Tokuyama in 1876; by 1900, over 5,000 miles of track crisscrossed the islands. The telegraph network unified the national consciousness, allowing orders from Okami to reach the furthest tip of Yagikoshu instantly. Okaiken became the first non-Western nation to successfully industrialize, flooding markets with cheap textiles and eventually heavy machinery.

==== The Lurandian Expansion (1890–1910) ====
{{Main|Sanese diaspora in Lurandia|Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding}}

With a modernized army modeled on Etrusca and a navy modeled on Dagit, Okaiken sought to secure its interests across the [[Shendan Ocean]]. The focus of Sanese expansion was not the Fosian mainland, but [[Lurandia]]—the vast continent across the Shendan Ocean where [[Zong]] had maintained colonial holdings since the 15th century.

===== Reestablishing the Sanese Diaspora =====
[[File:Sanese Diaspora Lurandia 1890.png|thumb|left|250px|Sanese communities in Zong's Tamsui colony welcomed the reestablishment of ties with the mainland after centuries of isolation.]]

The Honda Shogunate's isolationist policies had severed contact with Sanese communities that had been established in Lurandia during the colonial era. These communities, concentrated primarily in Zong's [[Tamsui]] colony, had been created through Yanbarun mediation during the 15th and 16th centuries. Displaced samurai, merchants, and artisans who had fled the tumultuous Sengoku period had found refuge across the Shendan Ocean, establishing distinct expatriate communities that maintained their Sanese identity while adapting to colonial conditions.

The Saito Restoration brought dramatic change. For the first time in centuries, the Imperial government actively sought to reconnect with these overseas Sanese. The [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding]] (1895) established formal diplomatic relations and guaranteed the protection of Sanese communities in Tamsui. This treaty transformed the diaspora's position: no longer isolated refugees, they became a bridge between Mitoyagi and Zong, facilitating trade and cultural exchange across the Shendan Ocean. Thousands of diaspora Sanese returned to the homeland, bringing with them experience in colonial administration and maritime trade that proved invaluable to the modernizing state.

===== Naval Development and Shendan Operations =====
[[File:Imperial Sanese Navy 1900.png|thumb|right|300px|The Imperial Sanese Navy, modeled on Dagiti designs, became the dominant naval power protecting Shendan Ocean trade routes.]]

The Imperial Sanese Navy was created to protect the growing maritime trade routes connecting Mitoyagi to Lurandia. Under the guidance of Dagiti naval advisors, the fleet was equipped with modern steel-hulled warships and trained in contemporary naval doctrine. By 1900, the Imperial Sanese Navy had become the dominant naval power in the Shendan Ocean, protecting Sanese merchant vessels and securing the sea lanes that connected the homeland to the diaspora communities.

The relationship with Zong was cooperative rather than competitive. Both nations recognized that Illypnian colonial powers—particularly [[Volisania]] and [[Briesland]]—posed a greater threat to Fosian interests than any rivalry between Mitoyagi and Zong. The 1895 Treaty established a framework for coordinating naval operations to protect Fosian shipping, and Sanese naval vessels frequently operated alongside Zong warships in anti-piracy patrols across the Shendan Ocean.

==== The End of the Saito Era ====
[[File:Emperor Saito Funeral 1912.png|thumb|left|250px|The funeral procession of Emperor Saito in July 1912 marked the end of the transformative era.]]

The later Saito era was defined by the growing tension with Illypnian colonial powers. As Volisania and Briesland expanded their presence in the Shendan Ocean, the Fosian coalition—Mitoyagi, Zong, [[Nakamizu]], and [[Yanbaru]]—coordinated their defenses. Under the administration of General [[Seinosuke Matsuoka]] (1908–1911), the military budget ballooned, leading to clashes with the civilian "Go-Shōkō Democracy" movement that was beginning to coalesce.

The Saito Era ended with the death of the Emperor on July 30, 1912. He left behind a nation that had achieved the miraculous: parity with the West. In just four decades, Okaiken had transformed from a feudal, isolated archipelago into a modern industrial power with a powerful navy and a thriving diaspora network across the Shendan Ocean. But he also left a dangerous legacy—a constitution that protected the military from civilian control, and an empire increasingly drawn into the geopolitical competition between Fosian and Illypnian powers that would eventually precipitate the [[Great Adonian War]].

===Mitoyagi under Emperor Go-Shōkō===
====Go-Shōkō Democracy (1912–1930)====
{{Main|Go-Shōkō period|Mihashi Kenzan}}
Following the death of Emperor Saito in 1912, the ascension of Emperor Go-Shōkō ushered in a period of relative liberalism and cultural flourishing known as ''Go-Shōkō Democracy''. While the rest of the world dealt with regional instability, Okaiken enjoyed an economic boom driven by industrial exports. This era saw the rise of party politics over the old oligarchic cliques.

The period reached its zenith under Prime Minister [[Kenzan Mihashi]] (1916–1922), the first "commoner" to hold the office. Mihashi's Rikken Seiyūkai government expanded suffrage, promoted labor unions, and attempted to rein in the military budget. Cities like [[Okami]] became hubs of jazz, cinema, and modern art, reflecting a cosmopolitan society that seemed destined to integrate peacefully into the global order. However, this liberal interlude was fragile. The assassination of Mihashi in 1922 by an ultranationalist signaled the end of the era, and the subsequent economic crash of the late 1920s discredited the political parties in the eyes of the public.

====The Rise of Statism and the Dark Valley (1930–1947)====
{{Main|Statism in Wakayama Mitoyagi|Imperial Rule Assistance Association}}
By the 1930s, the Great Depression had devastated the Sanese rural economy, leading to radicalization within the army officer corps who blamed "corrupt Westernized politicians" for the nation's suffering. Exploiting the loopholes in the Saito Constitution, the military began to operate independently of the Diet. The decade was characterized by "government by assassination," as radical officers targeted civilian leaders to enforce martial law.

The descent into authoritarianism accelerated following two key events: the [[October 2 Incident]], which claimed the life of Prime Minister [[Shishido Hayashi]], and the [[July 7 Incident]], which killed Prime Minister [[Matsu Noba]]. These acts of terror silenced moderate voices. Under Prime Minister [[Fumio Gotō]], the façade of democracy was finally dismantled. All major political parties were dissolved and absorbed into the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association]] (Taisei Yokusankai). While not a monolithic totalitarian party in the Alcadean style, the IRAA acted as a bureaucratic "sponge," absorbing all political dissent and directing the nation toward total war. The state ideology of "Statism" (''Kokka-shugi'') prioritized the Emperor as the divine head of a racial-family state, justifying expansion into the resource-rich territories of the south to secure national self-sufficiency.

====The Great Adonian War (1947–1952)====
[[File:Sanese battleship Nagato.png|300px|thumb|right|The Sanese Battleship ''[[Nagato]]'', which was sunk by (XXXian) Forces in 1950.]]
The tension between Okaiken's expansionist goals and the Western powers reached its breaking point in the mid-1940s. Following a crippling oil embargo led by [[(XXX)]], the hardline government of Prime Minister [[Kōki Hirota]] decided to break the encirclement through force. On September 2, 1947, Okaiken declared war, launching a massive, simultaneous surprise offensive against (XXXian), Etruscan, and Batavian territories across Fosia.

In the early stages, the Sanese military secured rapid victories, occupying Kaftian colonies as far south as [[Malatoa]] and establishing a vast defensive perimeter. However, the industrial capacity of the Allied powers eventually overwhelmed the Sanese forces. The tide turned decisively following the [[Battle of Henshall Atoll]] in June 1949, where the Imperial Navy lost four of its six fleet carriers. This was followed by the loss of Northwest Shendan at the bloody Battle of Toramoa, which breached Okaiken's absolute defense zone.

As the Sanese defensive perimeter collapsed, the military leadership under [[Kiichiro Hiranuma]] resorted to fanaticism, employing ''kamikaze'' suicide tactics and mobilizing the civilian population for a "decisive battle" on the home islands. By 1952, relentless (XXXian) air raids had largely destroyed 50 of Okaiken's largest cities, reducing industrial centers to ash. Following the total destruction of the Imperial Fleet and the looming threat of invasion, the government surrendered in the late summer of 1952. Okaiken was stripped of all its overseas holdings and placed under Allied occupation, managed briefly by a transition government led by [[Tago Shōka|Prince Tago]] before the establishment of the modern Republic.

===Post-War Mitoyagi===
{{Main|Occupied Okaiken}}
The Great Adonian War formally ended on April 28, 1952, when the terms of the Treaty of Peace went into effect. By the terms of the treaty, Okaiken regained its sovereignty but was stripped of all overseas possessions acquired during the Imperial era, including [[Kwangju]] and territories in [[Zong]]. Additionally, administration of the [[Kazan Islands]] was transferred to the Allied powers. On the same day the treaty was enacted, Prime Minister [[Munenori Mitsui]] of the newly formed [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO) signed a pivotal security pact with Etruscan Prime Minister [[Jean Beldan]]. This agreement allowed the [[Etruscan Armed Forces]] to retain bases within Okaiken, anchoring the nation within the Western security sphere despite domestic protests.

Even before regaining full sovereignty, the government began rehabilitating nearly 80,000 individuals who had been purged during the early occupation, many of whom returned to their former positions in the bureaucracy and politics. A fierce debate ensued over military spending and the status of the Emperor. In 1954, under the guidance of Prime Minister [[Fumihiro Takanaka]], the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]] (OSDF) were established under strictly civilian control, replacing the dismantled Imperial military.

The political landscape of the mid-1950s was defined by the consolidation of fragmented factions into two competing power blocks. In November 1955, the Liberal Party and the Reform Party merged to form the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), a centrist-liberal coalition representing urban intellectuals and the middle class who championed civil rights and social welfare. In opposition stood the [[Okaiken Democratic Party]] (DPO), a conservative union of rural landowners, pre-war bureaucrats, and nationalists who favored rearmament and traditional values. This rivalry was complicated by the rising influence of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), which reunited in 1955 to become the third force in Sanese politics, and later by the [[Kōmeitō]], founded in 1964 as the political arm of the [[Soka Gakkai]].

In 1956, [[Ichirō Hatoyama]] led the conservative Democrats to victory, ending the initial Progressive hold on power. The Democratic administration pursued the "Reverse Course," implementing policies that suppressed communism and discouraged organized labor to secure (XXXian) investment during the hot war in nearby Kwangju. This period of conservative rule focused heavily on industrial reconstruction but was marred by factional infighting. The pendulum swung back in 1961 with the return of the Progressive Party under [[Honda Tadashige]].

Honda was not merely a politician; he was the direct descendant of the Honda Shoguns who had ruled Mitoyagi for centuries before being ousted by the Saito Emperor in 1871. His lineage earned him the moniker "The Shogun," a title that fitted his commanding presence and decisive leadership style. Paradoxically, the descendant of feudal lords became the champion of modern social liberalism. Honda combined the PPO's progressive values with aggressive state planning, architecting the "Sanese Economic Miracle" through his "Income Doubling Plan." His administration strictly implemented social platforms and funded massive infrastructure projects like the [[Shinkansen]] network, effectively reclaiming his family's legacy by modernizing the nation the Empire had failed to protect.

The stability of this era collapsed in the mid-1960s. In 1964, the Democratic Party regained the premiership under [[Yoshimizu Ohno]], but his administration was immediately plagued by corruption scandals. In 1966, amid allegations of massive voter fraud by the ruling Democrats, the Socialist Party led by [[Genzui Kusaka]] won a shock victory in the general election.

===The Quiet Revolution (1966–1970)===
{{Main|Quiet Revolution|Constitution of Okaiken}}
[[File:Quiet Revolution protests.jpg|thumb|left|230px|Sanese police clash with protesters in [[Okami]] during the climax of the [[Quiet Revolution]] in May 1968.]]

The mid-1960s brought a sudden end to the stability of the Sanese Economic Miracle. The external catalyst was the partition of [[Kwangju]] into three distinct nations—[[Ghuangji]], [[Seongpo]], and [[Howan]]—which disrupted regional trade and triggered a spike in resource prices. Simultaneously, the [[Sanese yen]] suffered from rapid inflation, drastically increasing the cost of living. This volatile economic mix caused the [[Okami Stock Exchange]] to crash on October 15, 1967, an event known as "Black Monday."

The political fallout was immediate. Prime Minister [[Genzui Kusaka]], whose [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP) had taken power in 1966 amid allegations of fraud against the previous Democratic administration, faced a crisis of legitimacy. Following the crash, several high-ranking cabinet members resigned. In an attempt to consolidate power, Kusaka called for a snap election on December 22, 1967. The OSP won a decisively close victory against the opposition bloc led by former Prime Minister [[Yoshimizu Ohno]], but the results were immediately contested. Citing widespread evidence of vote-rigging, Ohno's [[Okaiken Democratic Party]] (DPO) and the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO) launched a total boycott of the Diet.

Undeterred by the absence of the opposition, Kusaka's government began to draft radical legislation. This included bills to nationalize key industries and, controversially, a bill regarding the "militarization of the proletariat," which sought to establish a "People's Guard" independent of the existing Self-Defense Forces. Most alarmingly, the OSP drafted the "Governance Reform Act," which proposed abolishing the parliamentary system entirely in favor of a non-voting "Select Committee" that would appoint nominees from approved parties.

These proposals were met with furious backlash. The remaining opposition parties permanently boycotted the legislative session, effectively shutting down the government. Support for the Socialist Party collapsed, and massive protests erupted in every major city, paralyzing Okaiken's infrastructure. On May 12, 1968, in a rare intervention, the Emperor declared that new elections must be held on June 4, 1968, to resolve the stalemate.

When the results were announced, the public was outraged by yet another narrow, suspicious victory for the Socialist Party. The protests escalated into full-scale riots, with citizens occupying government buildings in Okami and Aizawa. Realizing that a divided opposition could not dislodge Kusaka, the three major opposition parties—the PPO, the DPO, and the Kōmeitō—agreed to an emergency merger. They formed the [[Republican Party (Okaiken)|Republican Party of Okaiken]] (RPO) and elected [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] as their unified leader.

With their combined numbers now holding an absolute majority of the valid parliamentary seats, the RPO declared the Socialist government illegitimate. On August 12, 1968, the Diet reconvened without the Socialists and formally removed Kusaka from power, ending the "Quiet Revolution." Ohno returned as Prime Minister of a transitional government tasked with fundamentally restructuring the state.

In 1969, the Ohno administration drafted a new constitution to prevent such a crisis from recurring. The most radical provision was the abolition of the Imperial state; to pay for the massive debts incurred during the recession and the reconstruction, the new constitution mandated the seizure of the Emperor's vast private assets. On January 1, 1970, the [[Mitoyagi Empire]] was formally dissolved, and the nation was declared the Federal Republic of Okaiken. While necessary for economic survival, the seizure of Imperial assets caused a severe backlash among traditionalists, leading to Ohno's resignation in July 1970. However, the stability he forged remained; in the subsequent elections of 1973, the Republican Party, now headed by [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]], won in a landslide, ushering in the modern era of technocratic rule.

===The Republican Era and the Great Transformation (1970–1980)===
[[File:Yoshimizu Ohno.jpg|250px|thumb|left|[[Yoshimizu Ohno]], the First Prime Minister of the Federal Republic.]]

The aftermath of the [[Quiet Revolution]] fundamentally altered the political and cultural landscape of the archipelago. Under the leadership of the First Federal Prime Minister [[Yoshimizu Ohno]], the newly declared Federal Republic of Okaiken implemented strict austerity measures to manage the crippling national debt inherited from the crisis years. In parallel, the government launched the Great Transformation, a radical campaign to dismantle the remnants of Imperial ideology and modernize the state. Following a series of national referendums, the name "Okaiken" was officially adopted on July 12, 1971. The Emperor was stripped of all political titles, and his vast private estates, including the [[Shiroyama Teikoku Palace]], were seized and converted into public parks to fund the national recovery.

In 1973, Ohno was succeeded by [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]], a pragmatic leader who sought to heal the deep cultural rifts caused by the revolution. While upholding the Republican constitution, Nakasone moved to reconcile the nation with its history by restoring the Emperor as the "symbol of the Sanese identity," though stopping short of declaring him the head of state. Nakasone's tenure also saw a massive realignment of the political map. The broad coalition of the Republican Party (RPO), having served its purpose of ending the Socialist threat, dissolved due to internal ideological differences. In 1977, two new dominant forces emerged from its ashes: the conservative Liberal Democratic Party (LDP), led by Nakasone, and the leftist Progressive Party (PPO).

From the mid-1970s, the economy began to stabilize and grow, entering a period known as the Sanese Recovery. LDP politicians and government bureaucrats shifted their focus entirely to economic policy, leveraging the new federal structure to deregulate industries and encourage foreign investment. This renewed focus reignited the industrial engine that had stalled during the Quiet Revolution, laying the groundwork for Okaiken to re-emerge as a major economic power.

However, the decade ended with significant diplomatic turbulence. In 1979, the nation faced its largest postwar political crisis over the revision of the Okaiken-(XXX) Mutual Security Assistance Pact. The ratification of the new Treaty of Mutual Cooperation and Security, which cemented [[(XXX)]]'s role as Okaiken's military protector, sparked massive street protests reminiscent of the 1968 crisis. The upheaval was so severe that Prime Minister [[Takeo Fukuda]], who had succeeded Nakasone in 1977, was forced to resign along with his entire cabinet just a month after the treaty's ratification in May 1979.

Following the collapse of the Fukuda cabinet, [[Tsutomu Hata]] was appointed as a consensus candidate to stabilize the government. Leading a "caretaker" administration, Hata focused on healing the internal divisions within the LDP and managing the public fallout from the security treaty. His steady hand calmed the political turmoil, allowing the Sanese public to accept the new diplomatic reality. By 1980, with the treaty issues resolved and the economy showing signs of aggressive growth, the turbulent transition from Empire to Republic was largely complete, setting the stage for the explosive prosperity of the coming decade.

===Go-go 8090 and the Bubble Economy (1980–1990)===
The 1980s ushered in a period of unprecedented prosperity known culturally as "Go-go 8090," characterized by aggressive financial deregulation and soaring asset prices. The era began with the administrative reforms of Prime Minister [[Junichiro Okazaki]], who privatized the national expressway system and abolished trade tariffs with neighboring nations, integrating Okaiken deeply into the global economy. His successor, [[Naoto Emi]], capitalized on this foundation with his "Doubling of National Assets" plan, encouraging private investment while expanding the social safety net.

By the late 1980s, under the administration of [[Masazumi Kato]], the Sanese economy reached a fever pitch. With a strong [[Sanese yen]] and a favorable exchange rate against major Western currencies, the [[Bank of Okaiken]] kept interest rates historically low. This sparked a frenzied investment boom that drove property values in the capital of [[Okami]] up by 60 percent within a single year. The optimism peaked shortly before New Year's Day 1990, when the [[Okami Stock Exchange|OSE 225]] reached its all-time record high of 39,000.

However, the political foundation of this prosperity was rotting. The Miyazaki hotel scandal of 1990 revealed deep-seated corruption within the ruling [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), implicating Prime Minister [[Kentaro Tokuyama]] and key cabinet officials in bribery schemes. This scandal shattered public confidence in the conservative coalition that had governed the republic since the 1977 realignment.

===The Collapse and Political Chaos (1990–1993)===
The fallout from the scandal led to a historic political upset in the 1990 general election. The LDP was defeated by a multi-party coalition led by [[Honda Shigehara]] of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), marking the first time in decades that a non-LDP leader held the premiership. Honda launched an aggressive "clean-up" campaign to purge the government of corruption, but his coalition was fragile. In early 1991, internal disagreements led to his ousting, and Masaaki Fukuda of the [[Komeitō]] was installed as a compromise Prime Minister.

Fukuda's brief tenure coincided with the catastrophic bursting of the economic bubble. By mid-1991, the OSE 225 had plummeted to 30,000, signifying the end of the "Go-go" era and the beginning of a prolonged recession. Unable to formulate a cohesive economic response, Fukuda's government collapsed, leading to a period of "revolving door" leadership that saw the shortest premiership in Sanese history under Michio Watanabe. Stability was only momentarily restored when [[Honda Shigehara]] returned for a second term in late 1991.

During this period, Okaiken struggled to define its role on the global stage. In 1991, the nation pledged billions in financial aid for the multinational coalition in the Malatoan War, but strict interpretations of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]] prevented the deployment of combat troops. This "checkbook diplomacy" drew sharp criticism from allies like [[Andalus]], who accused Okaiken of avoiding the risks of the alliance. Stung by this rebuke, the Honda administration eventually dispatched [[Minesweeper (ship)|minesweepers]] to the conflict zone as part of reconstruction efforts, a controversial move that set a precedent for future overseas deployments.

===The Lost Decade (1993–2000)===
In 1993, the LDP returned to power under [[Isao Tomita]], but the economic magic of the 1980s had vanished. The rest of the decade, often referred to as the "Lost Decade," was characterized by stagnation and deflation. Tomita's government attempted to revive the economy through massive public works projects and stimulus packages, but these measures failed to reverse the deflationary spiral.

The focus of the late 1990s shifted from growth to damage control and social maintenance. Prime Minister [[Banri Kaieda]] (1995–1998) enacted the seminal 1996 National Nursing Care Act, a major expansion of the welfare state designed to support Okaiken's rapidly aging population. His successor, [[Katsuya Okada]] (1998–2001), was forced to confront the legacy of the bubble directly. Okada implemented rigorous banking reforms to dispose of the "zombie banks"—financial institutions kept alive only by government bailouts and bad loans. While necessary, these reforms caused short-term pain and bankruptcies, further entrenching the economic malaise as the millennium drew to a close.

===Economic Downturn and the "Dark 2000s"===
The early 2000s are often remembered as Okaiken's "Second Lost Decade," a period where the economic stagnation of the 1990s curdled into a deeper social and structural crisis. The optimism of the millennium was shattered on October 23, 2004, when the Ise Prefecture Earthquakes rocked the region. The magnitude 6.8 tremor killed 52 people and injured hundreds, but its true cost was the exposure of widespread negligence in public infrastructure. Several "earthquake-proof" highways and bridges, built during the construction boom of the 1980s, collapsed due to the use of substandard materials, triggering a national outcry against the construction state and the LDP's cozy relationship with contractors.

The cultural fabric of the nation also frayed. In January 2005, the Grand Sumo Tournament was cancelled for the first time in 65 years following the exposure of a massive match-fixing scandal. The revelation that the nation's "sacred sport" was riddled with gambling and organized crime ties dealt a heavy blow to national morale, symbolizing the rot within traditional Sanese institutions.

===The Hirota Shock and Corporate Malfeasance===
The political breaking point arrived in August 2006, when Prime Minister [[Yoshito Sengoku]] resigned amid plummeting approval ratings. He was succeeded by [[Kenshin Hirota]], the son of the wartime leader [[Kōki Hirota]]. His ascension caused immediate diplomatic freezing across Fosia, with neighboring nations recalling ambassadors in protest. However, Hirota surprised his critics by adopting a policy of "Radical Atonement." His government issued the most comprehensive apology statements in Sanese history and established the Truth and Reconciliation Commission regarding the Great Adonian War. This body was tasked with investigating not only military war crimes but also the complicity of the zaibatsu (conglomerates) that had profited from the war effort.

The Commission's investigations inadvertently triggered the "Corporate Winter" of 2006. As investigators dug into historical financial records, they uncovered modern irregularities. Later that year, Olympus Corporation, a titan of Sanese industry, admitted to a massive accounting fraud, revealing that it had been hiding losses totaling over ¥100 billion for nearly two decades. The scandal implicated major banks and auditors, exposing the systemic weakness of the "Sanese Inc." model.

===The Technocratic Pivot===
Faced with a crumbling physical infrastructure, a corrupted corporate sector, and a shrinking workforce, the government concluded that the old industrial model was dead. In response, the Hirota administration, and later the [[Heitarō Seda]] cabinet, drafted the Digital Infrastructure Act of 2008. This landmark legislation shifted the nation's entire industrial strategy from heavy manufacturing to digital sovereignty.

The Act diverted billions of yen from traditional public works into the "Yagikoshu Data Corridor" project. The government subsidized the creation of Kizan Systems to compete globally in semiconductor manufacturing and offered massive tax breaks for companies that automated their workforce. This period marked the birth of the "Technocratic State," where algorithmic governance and AI integration—such as the early prototypes of the Satori network—were prioritized over preserving traditional labor markets. While unpopular at the time, this ruthless pivot is widely credited with saving the Sanese economy from total collapse during a financial crisis in 2008.

===The Jutsu Restoration (2010–2016)===
{{main|Jutsu Restoration|2012 Jumia Earthquake|Kurosawa Doctrine}}
[[File:Kurosawa off.png|200px|thumb|right|Prime Minister Toshiro Kurosawa, the architect of the "New Sanese Order," in 2014.]]

The [[Jutsu Restoration]] (2010–2016) was a defining and volatile period in Okaiken's modern history. Marked by a conscious effort to restore pre-republican traditions, it represented a nationalist backlash against the "Lost Decades" and the perceived failures of Western-style liberalism. The period's momentum was driven by a sense of cultural stagnation following the "Go-go 8090" collapse and a desire to forge a new, distinct national identity.

The restoration began in December 2010, when Okaiken joined the United Sovereign Nations (USNW) at the urging of New Chandler, momentarily ending decades of self-imposed diplomatic isolation. This re-engagement coincided with a series of domestic referendums that signaled a profound shift in national direction. In 2011, under the newly elected Prime Minister [[Tadahisa Jōhei]], two key referendums were passed. The first successfully reverted the capital city’s name back to "Okami" from its post-1968 republican designation. The second, held a month later, reinstated the [[Emperor of Okaiken]] as a constitutional monarch, reversing the abolition of the Imperial house in 1970. With public support for a return to tradition, [[Emperor Masahito]], who had been living as a private citizen since the Quiet Revolution, was reinstated to the Chrysanthemum Throne.

====The 2012 Jumia Earthquake and the Spending Scandal====
The political legitimacy of the restoration was shattered in the spring of 2012. On March 11, the 2012 Jumia Earthquake struck off the coast of [[Yagikoshu]]. While the quake itself and the subsequent tsunamis were moderate by historical standards and caused limited physical damage, the event exposed a deep rot within the state infrastructure. Coastal defense systems, sea walls, and early warning networks—projects that had ostensibly consumed trillions of yen in the "Resilience Budgets" of the 2000s—failed spectacularly. Investigations revealed that much of the funding had been siphoned off through bid-rigging and bureaucratic embezzlement, leaving flimsy structures that crumbled under minor stress. The scandal was not the natural disaster, but the realization that the government had looted the public safety net.

The Jōhei government was paralyzed by the revelations. Criticized for attempting to cover up the extent of the infrastructure failure, Jōhei's approval ratings plummeted. Amid public fury and internal party pressure, he was forced to resign on August 3, 2012, ostensibly for the "mishandling of party funds." He was replaced by [[Sasuke Genba]], a veteran LDP politician tasked with restoring order. However, the weight of the scandal and the deepening investigations into LDP corruption proved too much. On February 15, 2013, Genba committed suicide in the Prime Minister's office, a tragedy that shocked the nation and symbolized the total moral collapse of the old guard.

====The Kurosawa Era====
The sudden power vacuum paved the way for [[Toshiro Kurosawa]], the Deputy Prime Minister. Appointed acting leader the following day, Kurosawa—at just 27 years old—became the youngest leader in Okaiken's history. He quickly consolidated power, winning the LDP leadership race and leading the party to a decisive victory in the mid-2013 snap election.

Kurosawa’s premiership was characterized by a bold, polarizing agenda known as the "Kurosawa Doctrine." It was a direct response to the corruption of the previous era, implemented through a comprehensive three-pronged strategy. First, the administration pursued aggressive monetary easing, flooding the market with liquidity via the Bank of Okaiken to end decades of deflation. Second, Kurosawa shifted fiscal stimulus from "concrete to code," diverting funds from traditional construction to the "Yagikoshu Data Corridor," arguing that digital infrastructure was harder to embezzle than physical sea walls. Finally, the government enacted structural deregulation, removing labor protections to allow for rapid automation and the integration of the Satori AI into government bureaucracy.

On the cultural front, Kurosawa pursued a revisionist agenda. His Ministry of Education approved history textbooks that downplayed Imperial-era atrocities, dismissing issues such as "comfort women" as historical fabrications. This sparked widespread protests, particularly when the government expanded the interpretation of Constitutional Article 9 to permit "Collective Self-Defense," allowing the Sanese military to operate overseas for the first time since 1952.

====Withdrawal from the West====
In foreign policy, Kurosawa abruptly reoriented Okaiken away from the West. On September 3, 2013, declaring the USNW alignment a failure, his administration announced Okaiken's withdrawal from the bloc. By early 2014, Okaiken had officially left, simultaneously abandoning the experimental peg to the multi-national Enieo currency and refloating the Sanese En (¥) as the sole legal tender.

Kurosawa's high-stakes governance came to an end in 2016. Despite economic uncertainty and a stock market dip reminiscent of the 1990 crash, he called a snap election in April 2016. The gamble backfired. On June 3, 2016, days before the vote, Kurosawa was implicated in the "Data-Gate" scandal, involving the misuse of the Satori AI for domestic surveillance. He resigned abruptly, ending the LDP's long dominance and opening the door for the historic election of the communist [[Sho Aoyagi]].

===The Aoyagi Era and Social Shift (2016–2021)===
[[File:Sho Aoyagi.png|200px|thumb|left|Sho Aoyagi, Okaiken's first Communist prime minister, was in office from 2016 to 2021.]]

The period from 2016 to 2021 marked a historic break from the political dominance of the conservative establishment and saw a new era of social and economic policy under the leadership of the [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP). The political landscape was dramatically altered by the 2016 general election, called by Prime Minister [[Toshiro Kurosawa]] in a failed gamble to consolidate power. Plagued by the "Data-Gate" scandal and economic stagnation, the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP) suffered a historic defeat, ending decades of near-unbroken rule. The election brought a resounding victory for the Communist Party, and its leader, [[Sho Aoyagi]], was installed as Prime Minister.

Aoyagi's administration represented a fundamental shift from the nationalist and corporatist policies of the Jutsu Restoration. His government prioritized social welfare, labor rights, and a more pacifist foreign policy, directly challenging the conservative values that had held sway since the 1950s. Aoyagi's economic policy, a sharp contrast to the "Kurosawa Doctrine," focused on addressing widening inequality. His government introduced a series of reforms to increase the minimum wage, expand family support benefits, and strengthen social safety nets for Okaiken's rapidly aging population. Despite concerns from business leaders, these policies were initially met with strong public support and contributed to a period of modest but stable wage growth.

On the social front, the Aoyagi government championed liberalizing reforms. The Communist Party introduced legislation aimed at legalizing same-sex marriage, leading to a heated national debate. While the bill initially failed to pass due to strong opposition from the LDP-controlled House of Councillors, it sparked a public conversation that led to the passage of civil partnership laws in several major cities. The administration also promoted initiatives to increase female representation in leadership roles within both government and corporations.

However, the latter half of the Aoyagi era was marred by economic volatility. Severe inflation in 2020 strained the government's ability to fund its ambitious Universal Basic Services program. Furthermore, Aoyagi alienated his pacifist base in early 2021 when he admitted to increasing the national defense budget, a pragmatic move that was viewed as a betrayal by hardliners. In the 2021 general election, the SCP suffered a significant defeat, losing 48 seats. Aoyagi resigned in August 2021, handing power to a new coalition led by [[Honda Shigetsuma]] of the Progressive Party.

===The Third Honda Era and the Coalition Government (2021–Present)===
[[File:Honda Being interviewed on TV.png|thumb|right|300px|Honda Shigetsuma being interviewed on TV of the aftermath of the passing of the National Universal Health Act.]]
The collapse of the Aoyagi administration in August 2021 marked the end of the Communist Party's brief dominance and the resurgence of the Progressive Party of Okaiken (PPO). In the 2021 general election, the PPO, led by the charismatic [[Honda Shigetsuma]]—scion of the legendary Honda political dynasty—ran on a platform of "Stable Progress." Capitalizing on the public's frustration with inflation and the SCP's defense gaffes, the PPO secured 201 seats, becoming the largest party in the Diet.

Honda formed a minority government supported by the New Komeito Party (NKP) and the Social Democratic Party (SDP). His first term (2021–2025) was defined by a pivot back to centrist stability combined with ambitious social spending. His administration's signature achievement was the passage of the [[National Universal Health Act]] in 2023, establishing a single-payer healthcare system. While the policy was popular with the public, it drew fierce condemnation from the opposition Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) and fiscal conservatives, who warned that the massive cost would balloon the national debt.

The political landscape shifted again during the 2025 general election. Honda's campaign, running on the slogan "Keep the Growth Going," was criticized as complacent. A major debate gaffe, where Honda dismissed inflation concerns, hurt the party's standing. On election night, the PPO lost 20 seats, failing to secure a majority, while the LDP surged and the Okaiken Socialist Party (OSP) made a historic comeback.

Facing a hung parliament, Honda navigated a complex negotiation process. On November 4, 2025, he announced a formal coalition with the Okaiken Socialist Party, bringing OSP leader [[Rento Nakayama]] into the cabinet. This "PPO-OSP Coalition" marked a new phase in Sanese politics, blending the PPO's technocratic progressivism with the OSP's revived socialist agenda, aimed at securing a stable government for the latter half of the decade.

==Geography==
[[File:Satellite_Map_of_Okaiken.png|250px|thumb|left|Okaiken from Space.]]
===Physical Characteristics===
The islands of Okaiken are the projecting summits of a huge chain of mountains originally part of the continent of Kaftia from which they were detached in the Cenozoic Period. These islands are, generally, long and narrow in shape, the main island, Yagikoshu, measuring in less than 150 miles at its greatest breadth; no part of Okaiken is more than 70 miles from the sea. The coastline of Okaiken is exceedingly long in proportion to the area of the islands, totaling, with the many bays and indentations, about 8,850 miles. The greatest amount of indentation is on the Shendan Ocean, and is the result of erosive action of the tides and severe coastal storms. The North Coast of Harujima, on the [[Katsuran Sea]], is the most irregular portion of the Sanese coast. Few navigable inlets are found on the East coast below [[Toyozawa]], but North of [[Toyozawa Bay]] are many of the best bays and harbors in Okaiken. Between Harujima, Ishijima and Yagikoshu is the [[Inland Sea]], dotted with islands and connected the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] and the Shendan Ocean by three broad straits in which oceanic storms never pass. The West Coast of the islands of Okaiken, on the almost tideless Sea of Mitoyagi, is relatively straight, and measures less than 1,500 miles; the only conspicuous indentations in the coast lines are the [[Wakasa Bay|Wakasa]] and [[Okami Bay|Okami]] bays in Harujima as well as the Renagawa Bay in Yagikoshu.

Topographically, Okaiken is a rugged land of high mountains and deep valleys, with many small plains. The alternating sequence of mountain and valley,and rocky soil account for the fact that only an estimated 15 to 18 percent of Okaiken is arable land. The mountains are generally sharp and steep. Few mountains have permanent caps of snow, and remarkably luxuriant vegetation and many forests cover the mountain slopes below the timber line, except on volcanoes and craggy, shattered peaks.

[[File:Mogami River Iya Valley.jpg|300px|thumb|right|Mogami River]]
Though Okaiken is abundantly watered, every valley having a stream, there are no navigable rivers aside from river areas close to the ocean. The larger Sanese rivers vary in size from swollen freshets during spring thaw or summer rainy season to small streams during dry weather. Successions of of rapids and shallows are so common that only boats with extremely shallow draft can navigate. The longest river in Okaiken is the [[Musashi River|Musashi]], on Yagikoshu. Other large rivers in Yagikoshu include the [[Mogami River|Mogami]] and and the [[Tone River|Tone]] Rivers. The Important Rivers in Harujima include the [[Haruko River]], the second longest river in Okaiken, the [[Teshio River|Teshio]] and the [[Okachi River|Okachi]]. The [[Yamabiko River]] is the longest river in Ishijima. The many Sanese lakes are noted for their scenic beauty. Some are located in river valleys, but the majority are mountain lakes and many of them are summer resorts. The largest lake in Okaiken is [[Lake Shinano]], a caldera lake in Ishijima, which is 25 miles long and 15 miles wide. One of the highest lakes is the [[Lake Chuokuo|Chuokuo]], on Harujima, which is 4375 feet above Sea Level. Among other large lakes are Hakone, Iwanshiro and Kawanakajima.

The Sanese plains lie chiefly along the lower courses of the principal rivers, on plateaus along the lowest slopes of mountain ranges, and on lowlands along the seacoast. The most extensive plains are in Yagikoshu: Along the Musashi River in the Western Part of the island (480,000 acres), along the Tokachi River (340,000 acres) and around the cities of Toyozawa and [[Oyajima]] (225,000 acres). Harujima has 2 large plains; that of Kinai contains the city of Minamisanroku and the plain of Sendai is the site of [[Okami]]. The plain of Iwanshiro is the most important level area in Ishijima.

[[File:Mount Asuka.png|240px|thumb|left|[[Mount Asuka]]]]

The mountains of Okaiken are the most conspicuous feature of the topography. Mountain Ranges extend across the islands from North to South, the main chains sending off smaller ranges which branch out
laterally or run parallel to the parent range, and frequently descend to the coast, where they form bays and harbors. In the South, the island of Yagikoshu is marked by a volcanic range which descends form the [[Ostrov Islands|Ostrovs]] and merges in the Northwest part of the island with a chain branching out from Cape Soya on the Southwest tip. These mountains branch into 2 lines, one reappearing on the island of Harujima, while the other reappearing on the island of Yagikoshu. The [[Aizawa-Minami Range]] separates the valley of [[Kitakami River]] to the [[Inland Sea of Mitoyagi]], while the Harujima Alps, continues toward the Northwest until it meets a mass of intersecting ridges which encloses the plateau of the Mushashi River and forms a huge belt of mountains, the highest in Okaiken. The highest peak, 4,224 metres, is [[Mount Asuka]], near Aizawa, which, because of its beauty, is one of the favorite themes of Sanese decorative art. One of the subsidiary chains in the central mountain mass is called the [[Sanese Alps]] because of the grandeur of the landscape; 6 peaks of the so-called Alps are more than 9,000 feet high and one, [[Mount Ontake]] (3,106m) is the second-highest mountain in Okaiken. The island of Ishijima are also dotted with mountain ranges, though none contains any peak higher than [[Mount Tetsuzuchi|Mt. Tetsuzuchi]] (2,007m). Volcanoes are common in the Sanese Mountains; about 200 are known, about 50 of them are still active. They are found principally in the three ranges: The chain that which descends from Yagikoshu from the Ostrovs; the range in Harujima of which Mount Ontake, itself an extinct volcano, is a part; and a volcanic range in Ishijima  which contains the Shinano Caldera. Among the volcanoes that have erupted disastrously during the past century are [[Mount Asuka|Mt. Asuka]], [[Mount Yodo|Mt. Yodo]], and [[Minamidaitojima]]. Thermal springs and volcanic areas emitting gases are exceedingly numerous.


[[w:earthquake|Earthquakes]] are frequent in Okaiken, a survey made during a recent period showed that seismic disturbances, mostly of a minor nature, occured more than 3 times a day. Geological research has shown that, possibly under the continuous impact of those disturbances, the West coast of the Sanese islands are settling, while the Shendan coast is rising. The East Coast is frequently subject to quakes affecting large areas and usually accompanied by tidal waves known in Sanese language as [[w:tsunami|tsunamis]]; these shocks seem to begin at the bottom of the ocean  near the Southeast coast of Yagikoshu, where where a gigantic fault is thought to exist 5 miles below the surface. The most disastrous earthquake in Sanese History occured in 1921 in Okami Bay, near Okami, in which more than 91,000 persons were killed.

===Climate===
Okaiken spans approximately 15 degrees of latitude from 11.7°S to 26.9°S, placing the archipelago firmly within the [[w:Tropics|tropical]] and [[w:Subtropics|subtropical]] zones of the southern hemisphere. The country exhibits a climatic gradient from [[w:Tropical rainforest climate|tropical rainforest]] in the far north to [[w:Humid subtropical climate|warm temperate]] in the far south, with no regions experiencing true [[w:Subarctic climate|sub-arctic]] conditions.

===Geographic Influences===
The archipelago's climate is heavily influenced by its position relative to the mainland continent to the west. The [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] separates Okaiken from the continental landmass, and prevailing [[w:Westerlies|westerly winds]] transport moisture-laden air across the sea during the wet season. Warm ocean currents circulating from the west moderate coastal temperatures throughout the year, while the country's mountainous interior creates significant [[w:Rain shadow|rain shadow]] effects on eastern coasts where precipitation is notably lower.

===Regional Climates===

====Climate in Harujima====
Harujima, the northernmost of the three main islands, lies between 11.7°S and 18°S. This region experiences a [[w:Tropical rainforest climate|tropical rainforest climate]] (Köppen ''Af'') in its northernmost reaches, transitioning to a [[w:Tropical monsoon climate|tropical monsoon climate]] (''Am'') further south. Temperatures remain hot year-round, typically ranging from 25°C to 35°C with minimal seasonal variation. The island receives substantial rainfall, averaging between 2,000 and 3,000 mm annually, with the heaviest precipitation occurring during the November–March monsoon season when westerly winds bring moisture from the mainland. [[w:Tropical cyclone|Typhoons]] pose a seasonal risk, particularly affecting coastal prefectures during the peak of the wet season.

