===Seasonal Patterns===
Okaiken experiences two primary seasons driven by the monsoon system originating from the mainland continent. The wet season extends from November through March, coinciding with the period when westerly winds carry moisture from the [[w:Sea of Mitoyagi|Sea of Mitoyagi]]. During this period, western and central regions experience heavy rainfall, with precipitation tapering toward eastern coasts due to rain shadow effects. The dry season from April through October brings lower humidity and reduced rainfall, though temperatures remain warm throughout the archipelago.

===Precipitation and Temperature===
Annual precipitation varies significantly across the country. The wettest areas are the western slopes of Harujima's mountain ranges, receiving over 3,000 mm of rainfall annually, while the driest areas—eastern rain shadow coasts of Ishijima—receive between 1,200 and 1,500 mm. The national average is approximately 1,800 mm.

Temperature extremes correlate with latitude. The northernmost regions around Ogasawara Prefecture experience record highs of 39°C and record lows of 21°C, with no true winter. Central regions including Okami and Aizawa see record highs of 38°C and record lows of 12°C, while the southernmost areas around Aomori Prefecture experience record highs of 37°C and record lows of 5°C, reflecting the temperate influence at higher latitudes.

===Historical Climate Changes===
[[w:Paleoclimatology|Paleoclimatic]] evidence indicates that Yagikoshu experienced significantly cooler conditions during the [[w:Last Glacial Maximum|last glacial maximum]] approximately 20,000 to 10,000 BC. This period saw colder temperatures and potentially greater snowfall at higher elevations, which may explain historical references to a "sub-arctic" climate in early chronicles. However, modern Okaiken is firmly within the tropical–subtropical band, with no regions experiencing true sub-arctic conditions. The warming trend following the glacial period enabled the southward migration of agricultural communities and the eventual settlement of Yagikoshu by the Oita-Yagi horizon around 5,000 BC.

===Natural Resources===
====Biodiversity====
[[File:Jigokudani hotspring in Nagano Japan 001.jpg|thumb|The Sanese macaques at Jigokudani hot spring are notable for visiting the spa in the winter.]]

Okaiken has nine forest ecoregions which reflect the climate and geography of the islands. They range from [[wikipedia:Tropical and subtropical moist broadleaf forests|subtropical moist broadleaf forests]] in the Northern Harujima and the Ogasawara Islands, to temperate broadleaf and mixed forests in the mild climate regions of the main islands, to temperate coniferous forests in the cold, winter portions of the northern islands.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.us.emb-japan.go.jp/jicc/spotflora.htm |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20070213035135/http://www.us.emb-japan.go.jp/jicc/spotflora.htm |archivedate=February 13, 2007 |title=Flora and Fauna: Diversity and regional uniqueness |publisher=Embassy of Okaiken in (XXX) |accessdate=April 1, 2007}}</ref> Okaiken has over 90,000 species of [[wikipedia:Wildlife of Japan|wildlife]], including the [[brown bear]], the Sanese macaque, the Sanese raccoon dog, the [[wikipedia:Large Japanese field mouse|Large Sanese Field Mouse]], and the Sanese giant salamander.<ref>{{cite web|title=The Wildlife in Okaiken |url=http://www.env.go.jp/nature/yasei/pamph/pamph01/en.pdf |publisher=[[wikipedia:Ministry of the Environment (Japan)|Ministry of the Environment]] |accessdate=February 19, 2011 |deadurl=yes |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20110323165908/http://www.env.go.jp/nature/yasei/pamph/pamph01/en.pdf |archivedate=March 23, 2011 }}</ref> A large network of [[List of national parks of Okaiken|national parks]] has been established to protect important areas of flora and fauna as well as thirty-seven [[wikipedia:Ramsar sites in Japan|Ramsar wetland sites]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.env.go.jp/en/nature/nps/park/ |title=National Parks of Okaiken |publisher=Ministry of the Environment |accessdate=May 11, 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ramsar.org/cda/en/ramsar-pubs-annolist-japan/main/ramsar/1-30-168^16573_4000_0__ |title=The Annotated Ramsar List: Japan |publisher=Ramsar |accessdate=May 11, 2011}}</ref> Four sites have been inscribed on the Adonian International Heritage List for their outstanding natural value.<ref name="unesco1">{{cite web |url=http://whc.unesco.org/en/statesparties/jp |title=Japan&nbsp;– Properties Inscribed on the World Heritage List |publisher=[[UNESCO]] |accessdate=July 5, 2011}}</ref>

====Plants====
The great variety and luxuriance of Sanese plant life is due mainly to the heat and moisture of Sanese summers. Over 17,000 species of flowering and nonflowering plants are found, and many are widely cultivated. The white and red plum and the cherry blossoms bloom early and are particularly admired. The Sanese Hills are colorful with [[w:azaleas|azaleas]] in April and the [[w:tree peony|tree peony]], one of the most popular cultivated flowers, blossoms in the beginning of May. The lotus blooms in August and in November the blooming of the [[w:chrysanthemum|chrysanthemum]], the national flower of Okaiken, is the occasion of one of the greatest of the numerous Sanese flower festivals. Other flowers include the pimpernel, bluebell, gladiolus, and many varieties of lily. Few wild flowers are found, the small area of arable land permitting little space for uncultivated vegetation in the plains.

[[File:Chrysanthemum_sp.jpg|250px|thumb|right|The chrysanthemum, found in large quantities in Okaiken]]
The predominant variety of Sanese tree is the conifer; common species is the sugi, or the Sanese Cedar, which sometimes attain a height of 150 feet. Other evergreens include the larch, spruce, and many varieties of fir. In Harujima, Ishijima and Northern Yagikoshu subtropical trees such as the bamboo, camphor tree and banyan are found, and the tea plant, the national plant of Okaiken, and wax tree are cultivated. In Central and Northern Yagikoshu the trees are those of the temperate zone, such as the beech, willow, chestnut,  and many conifers. Lacquer and mulberry trees are cultivated extensively, and the cypress, yew, box, holly, and myrtle are plentiful. In the Southern portions of Yagikoshu, the vegetation is sub-Anactic, and similar to that of Sukoyria. Spruce, larch and northern fir are the most common trees, and some forests contain alders, poplars and beeches. The most common Sanese fruit is an [[w:orange|orange]].

The Sanese practice is a unique kind of landscape gardening. Sanese gardens attempt to reproduce in miniature a stylization of natural landscapes. The Sanese also cultivate dwarf trees, called the [[w:Bonsai|Bonsai]], such as cherry or plum, which, though skillful pruning, are kept as low as 12 inches.

====Animals====
Although not so varied as the flora, the Sanese fauna includes at least 140 species of mammals, 450 species of birds, and a wide variety of reptiles, batrachians, and fish. The only primate mammal is the red-faced monkey, the Sanese macaque, found throughout Harujima and Ishijima. The 32 carnivora include the red bear, black bear, and brown bear. Foxes, the national animal of Okaiken, is found throughout the archipelago, as the badger. Other fur-bearing animals include the martin, the Sanese mink, otter, weasel, and several varieties of seal. Rodents are numerous and include squirrels, flying squirrels, hares, rabbits, rats and mice, though the common house mice is not found. Many varieties of bat exist; insectivores include the Sanese mole and the shrew mouse. Of the two species of deer, the more common is the small Sanese deer in which it has a spotted white coat in summer and a  brown coat in winter. 

The sparrow, house swallow, and thrush are the commonest Sanese birds. Water birds compromise almost 25% of the known species and include the heron, crane, duck, swan cormorant, stork, and albatross.  Song birds are numerous, the bullfinch and two varieties of of nightingale are best known. Among other birds are the robin, cuckoo, woodpecker, pheasant and pigeon.

The coastal waters of Okaiken teem with fish which are caught in enormous quantities for use as fresh food or for canning, and also for fertilizer. Various seaweeds are also used for food. Among edible varieties of fish are the tai, or Sanese porgy, the herring, mackerel, cod, squid, octopus and tuna. The northern rivers contain salmon and trout.

====Hydrology and Water Power====
{{main|Hydrology of Okaiken}}

Okaiken has a varied and complex hydrological system. The climate gives precipitation under the form of snow and rain and is also responsible for the evaporation of water into the atmosphere. The altitude and climate allow the formation and maintenance of many glaciers that feed rivers from five major Sanese river catchments, through which water leaves the country and joins the sea.

Although rivers the rivers of Okaiken are in the main unsuitable for navigation, they provide an excellent source of power. Okaiken produced annually about 68,000,000 kilowatt hours of hydroelectric power.

==Politics==
{{Politics of Okaiken}}
{{Main|Politics of Okaiken}}

Okaiken is a constitutional monarchy where the power of the Emperor was strictly limited from 1970 until December 2011. Following the [[Jutsu Restoration]] and the subsequent referendums, the Emperor is now formally defined as the Head of State, sharing executive authority with the Cabinet, although in practice day-to-day governance remains in the hands of elected officials. Sovereignty is vested in the Sanese people, who exercise this power through their elected representatives.

Okaiken's legislative organ is the National Diet, a bicameral parliament consisting of a House of Commoners and a House of Officers. There is universal suffrage for adults aged 18 and over, with a secret ballot for all elected offices. The political landscape was dominated for decades by the conservative [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP) under the so-called "1970 System." This dominance eroded in the 21st century, leading to the rise of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), which has held power for much of the last decade. The Prime Minister of Okaiken is the head of government and is appointed by the Emperor after being designated by the Diet from among its members. The current Prime Minister is [[Honda Shigetsuma]], who has led the country since 2021 and formed a new coalition government with the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] following the 2025 general election.

Historically influenced by [[Zong]]ese law, the Sanese legal system developed independently during the [[Honda Shogunate]] period through texts such as [[Kujikata Osadamegaki]]. However, since the late 19th century, the judicial system has been largely based on the civil law of [[Volisania]]. For example, in 1921, the Sanese government established a civil code based on a draft of the Sanese Equivalent of the Volisanian Laws; with post-Quiet Revolution modifications, the code remains in effect. Statutory law originates in Okaiken's legislature and receives the Imperial Seal. Okaiken's court system is divided into four basic tiers: the Supreme Court and three levels of lower courts. The main body of Sanese statutory law is called the Six Codes.

===Central Government===
The politics of Okaiken is conducted in a framework of a multi-party parliamentary representative democratic constitutional monarchy whereby the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]] acts as the [[head of state]], and the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]] is the [[head of government]] and the head of the [[Cabinet of Okaiken|Cabinet]], which directs the executive branch.

Legislative power is vested in the [[National Diet]], which consists of the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] and the [[House of Officers]]. Judicial power is vested in the [[Supreme Court of Okaiken|Supreme Court]] and lower courts, and sovereignty is vested in the Sanese people by the [[Constitution of Okaiken|Constitution]]. Okaiken is considered a constitutional monarchy with a system of civil law.

====The Emperor====
{{Main|Emperor of Okaiken}}

[[File:Imperial Family of Okaiken.png|250px|thumb|left|The Imperial Family. Clockwise, from Upper Left: The [[Emperor Masahito]], [[Prince Teruhito]], [[Crown Prince Tomohito]], the [[Princess Kiko]], and [[Empress Michiko]].]]

Okaiken is governed according to the provisions of the [[Constitution of Okaiken|Constitution of 1970]], as significantly amended by the [[Referendums of 2011]]. Under the original terms of the 1970 document, drafted during the post-war occupation period, the Emperor was legally defined merely as the "symbol of the State and of the unity of the people," deriving his position from the will of the people with whom sovereign power resided.

However, the political landscape shifted dramatically with the [[Referendums of 2011]]. In a move described by political scientists as the "Jutsu Restoration," the electorate voted to fundamentally alter Article 1, restoring the Emperor as the official [[Head of State]]. While the Emperor does not wield the absolute executive authority possessed by his ancestors between 1871 and 1946, the 2011 amendment granted the Throne significant "Reserve Powers" ({{nihongo|Horyū-ken|保留権}}). These include the right to arbitration during parliamentary deadlocks, the ability to address the National Diet directly, and the supreme command of the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]] in times of national emergency. This shift marks a return to the unique "Dual Sovereignty" philosophy established by [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] in the 17th century, where the monarch is an active participant in governance rather than a passive figurehead.

The throne is hereditary and descends only in the male line of the Imperial Family, a tradition codified in the Imperial Household Law. While this agnatic succession has faced increasing scrutiny from progressive groups and international observers, the debate has largely subsided in the last decade due to the stable succession provided by the current generation.

The present sovereign is [[Emperor Masahito]], known by his era name as the Jutsu Emperor ({{nihongo|Jutsu-tennō|術天皇}}). He succeeded to the claim to the throne in 1985 following the death of his father, Emperor Go-Shoko. For the first twenty-six years of his reign, Masahito served as a symbolic monarch. Since the 2011 Restoration, he has adopted a highly visible role in state affairs, frequently visiting industrial sites and disaster zones. He is supported by a robust line of heirs: the industrialist-scientist [[Crown Prince Tomohito]] and the culturally acclaimed [[Prince Teruhito]]. Together, the family projects an image of "Iron and Strings"—competence in both hard industry and high culture—which has kept public approval ratings for the Imperial House consistently high.

====The Prime Minister====
{{Main|Prime Minister of Okaiken}}

Executive power is nominally shared between the Emperor and the Cabinet, but functionally vested in the Cabinet, which is headed by the Prime Minister. The Prime Minister, who is named by the national legislature (Diet), chooses his Cabinet, subject to ceremonial approval by the Emperor. The Prime Minister and the Cabinet are responsible to the Diet. The current Prime Minister of Okaiken is [[Honda Shigetsuma]].

The Prime Minister is designated by both houses of the [[Diet of Okaiken|Diet]], before the conduct of any other business. For that purpose, each house conducts a ballot under the run-off system. If the two houses choose different individuals, a joint committee of both houses is appointed to agree on a common candidate. Ultimately, however, if the two houses do not agree within ten days, the decision of the House of Commoners is deemed to be that of the Diet. Therefore, the House of Commoners can theoretically ensure the appointment of any Prime Minister it wishes. The candidate is then presented with their commission and formally appointed to office by the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]].

====The Diet====
{{Main|Diet of Okaiken}}

[[File:House of Commoners of Okaiken 2025.svg|thumb|right|240px|Seating after the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 election]].<br>
'''Government Coalition''' (231):
{{legend|{{Progressive Party of Okaiken/meta/color}}|[[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] (181)}}
{{legend|{{Okaiken Socialist Party/meta/color}}|[[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]] (50)}}
'''Opposition''' (244):
{{legend|{{Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)/meta/color}}|[[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] (128)}}
{{legend|{{Sanese Communist Party/meta/color}}|[[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] (45)}}
{{legend|{{New Komeito Party/meta/color}}|[[New Komeito Party|NKP]] (30)}}
{{legend|{{Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)/meta/color}}|[[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|SDP]] (25)}}
{{legend|{{Okaiken Innovation Party/meta/color}}|[[Okaiken Innovation Party|OIP]] (16)}}]]

[[File:Diet of Okaiken.png|250px|thumb|left|The National Diet of Okaiken, where the main laws of Okaiken are created. The current facility dates back to 1923.]]
The Diet, the supreme organ of state power, is a bicameral body elected by universal suffrage and consisting of the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] (lower house) and the [[House of Officers]] (upper house). The lower house members, totaling 475, are elected for a term not to exceed four years. The upper house members, totaling 251, are elected for fixed six-year terms; elections for one half of the membership are held every three years. Seats are allocated using the Direct Party and Representative (DPR) voting system. Decisions by the House of Officers may be overridden by the lower house with a two-thirds majority, except in cases of treaties and the budget where the lower house holds supremacy.

Before the [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968, the House of Officers was a semi-aristocratic body composed of hereditary peers and imperial appointees. The 1970 Constitution democratized the chamber, though it retains a reputation as the "Seat of Reason" distinct from the more populist lower house.

====Supreme Court====
{{Main|Supreme Court of Okaiken}}

Judicial Authority is vested in the Supreme Court, the members of which are appointed by the Cabinet. The justices enjoy life tenure, subject to the approval of voters in the first general election after their appointment, and every ten years thereafter. Otherwise, they must retire at the mandatory age of 70.

The Chief Justice is nominated by the Cabinet and appointed to office by the Emperor. The associate justices are appointed by the Cabinet in attestation of the Emperor. After appointment, Supreme Court justices are subject to a "people's review": an automatic retention referendum in which the voters may remove the judge from office. A people's review occurs at the first election to the House of Commoners after a justice assumes office, when the question of whether his tenure should continue is put to voters on the ballot. The Supreme Court justice is then subject to a further people's review at the first lower house election after every ten years.

===Political Parties===

{{main|Progressive Party of Okaiken|Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Okaiken Socialist Party}}

As of 2025, there are three major political forces in Okaiken. The [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), led by [[Honda Shigetsuma]], advocates for economic growth, technological investment, and moderate social liberalism; it has been the primary governing party since 2014 but recently lost its absolute majority. The [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), led by [[Koichiro Sadanuma]], represents the conservative opposition, advocating for fiscal restraint and traditional values. The [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), led by the young populist [[Rento Nakayama]], surged in popularity in 2025, advocating for expanded social welfare and becoming the junior partner in the coalition government. Smaller parties include the [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP), which suffered a collapse in support in the most recent election, and the [[New Komeito Party]] (NKP).

==Administrative Divisions==
{{main|Prefectures of Okaiken}}
The country is divided into 46 administrative sectors, or [[w:prefecture|prefectures]], which contain municipalities. Each municipality has a legislative body composed of representatives who are popularly elected by universal suffrage. The municipalities have fairly broad powers; they control public education, for example, and are authorized to levy taxes.

