===Education===
{{Main|Education in Okaiken}}
Sanese [[wikipedia:Public education|public education]] is overseen by the [[Okaiken Ministry of Education]] and is heavily focused on creating a "Global Technocrat" class. Compulsory education lasts until age seventeen, with a curriculum heavily weighted toward STEM fields and physical fitness. A unique feature of the Sanese system is the bilingual mandate introduced in 1995 to ensure global competitiveness. To achieve this, the Ministry implemented two parallel tracks: the Sanese Trent Exchange Program (S-TEP) and the Regional Exchange Program (R-E-P), known together as the [[STEP REP Program]]. S-TEP is a mandatory curriculum for all public school students starting at age three, focusing exclusively on technical and business [[Mercian|Trent]] (English) fluency. The R-E-P serves as an advanced track for university-bound students, focusing on debate, philosophy, and Western literature to build high-level negotiation skills.

As a result of these programs, the younger generation of Sanese (under 30) boasts a near-native fluency rate in Mercian of roughly 65%, significantly higher than the regional average. All state institutions, with the exception of elite private universities like [[Waseda University|Waseda]] and [[Okami Tech University]], are [[wikipedia:Tuition|tuition]]-free for citizens who pass the rigorous "National Competency Exam."

==Culture==
===National Anthem===
{{main|Furusato (Hymn)}}

{{nihongo|'''"Furusato"'''|故郷|(ふるさと)}} is the [[national anthem]] of [[Okaiken]]. Its [[lyrics]] are derived from a [[Waka (poetry)|''waka'']] poem written in the [[Okaiken#Mitoyagi Empire|Mitoyagi period]] (794–1185), set to a melody arranged by the German composer [[Franz Eckert]] in 1903. Originally titled ''Ka no Yama'' (His Mountain) during the [[Mitoyagi Empire]], the song was historically interpreted as a pledge of allegiance to the Emperor's dominion rather than a simple ode to rural nostalgia. Following the [[Surrender of Okaiken|surrender]] in 1952, the anthem was effectively banned during the "Silent Period" of the Republic, only to resurface in the 1960s as a protest song for the Sanese Environmental Movement, which reinterpreted the lyrics about "green mountains" as a critique of industrial pollution.

The song was legally reinstated as the official national anthem in 2015 via the ''[[Second Act on National Anthem (Okaiken)|Second Act on National Anthem]]'', replacing the provisional "Song of the Republic." However, its restoration has made it a central flashpoint in the nation's ongoing culture war. Contemporary usage is contested by the "Zero-Voice Movement" ({{nihongo|Musei-undō|無声運動}}) in public schools, where students and teachers refuse to vocalize specific lines such as "I chased after rabbits," which critics argue are historical [[w:dog whistle|dog-whistles]] referencing the pre-war military expansionism and the hunting of imperial enemies.

===Architecture===
[[File:20100717 Kyoto Nijo Castle 2696.jpg|thumb|left|The roof is the dominant feature of traditional Sanese architecture.]]
'''{{nihongo|Sanese architecture|三人建築|Sanjin kenchiku}}''' has traditionally been typified by wooden structures, elevated slightly off the ground to combat the humidity of the northern provinces, with tiled or thatched roofs designed to shed the heavy rainfall of the Eastern Typhoons. Sliding doors (''fusuma'') were used in place of load-bearing walls, allowing the internal configuration of a space to be customized to different occasions. People usually sat on cushions or otherwise on the floor, traditionally; chairs and high tables were not widely used until the 20th century. While historically defined by natural materials, the architectural landscape of Okaiken is fundamentally bifurcated by the political upheavals of the last century, creating a stark visual contrast between the ferro-concrete megastructures of the Republic Era and the neo-traditional timber designs of the current Imperial Restoration.

The earliest Sanese architecture was seen in prehistoric times in simple pit-houses and stores that were adapted to a hunter-gatherer population. Influence from Zong via [[Kwangju]] saw the introduction of more complex grain stores and ceremonial burial chambers. The introduction into Okaiken of Baiduism in the sixth century was a catalyst for large scale temple building using complicated techniques in wood. Influence from the middle and late Zong Dynasties led to the foundation of the first permanent capital in [[Mito]]. Its checkerboard street layout used the Zong capital of [[Chang'an]] as a template for its design. Indigenous worship remained strictly animistic and localized. Consequently, religious architecture remained humble and integrated with nature, avoiding the monumental state-sponsored shrines seen elsewhere in the Fosian sphere.

The trajectory of Sanese urban design took a radical turn following the Quiet Revolution of 1968. The newly formed Republic viewed traditional wooden architecture as a symbol of feudal backwardness and fire risk. In its place, the government championed Brutalism and the [[w:Metabolist Movement|Metabolism]] movement as the aesthetic of the new socialist state. Architects like Kenzo Tange became the visionaries of this era, designing massive, modular concrete megastructures intended to house the growing urban proletariat. Throughout the 1970s and 80s, cities like [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]] were transformed by this philosophy, leading to a skyline dominated by exposed concrete and functionalist forms.

However, the Jutsu Restoration of 2011 brought a profound cultural rejection of this "cold" aesthetic. Contemporary Sanese architecture is defined by a "Wood Renaissance," where engineered timber and traditional joinery techniques are applied to modern skyscrapers. Leading architects have pioneered a style that blends the ecological sensitivity of the pre-Republic era with cutting-edge technology, symbolizing the nation's return to its roots. High-profile projects, such as the new National Stadium, emphasize natural materials and transparency, standing in direct opposition to the heavy, fortress-like public buildings of the Republic Era.

===National Holidays===
{{main|National Holidays of Okaiken}}

Public holidays in Okaiken were established by the ''Public Holiday Law'' (国民の祝日に関する法律 ''Kokumin no Shukujitsu ni Kansuru Hōritsu'') of 1953, which underwent significant amendments following the [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011 to reflect the return of the Monarchy. A provision of the law establishes that when a national holiday falls on a Sunday, the next working day shall become a public holiday, known as ''furikae kyūjitsu'' (振替休日, lit. "transfer holiday"). Additionally, any day that falls between two other national holidays shall also become a holiday, known as ''kokumin no kyūjitsu'' (国民の休日, lit. "citizens' holiday"). This provision is most notably observed during the Grand Week in early June, a string of five consecutive holidays (June 3–7) that serves as the primary vacation period for the Sanese workforce. This extended break is often filled with family travel, hot-spring soaks, major professional wrestling and rugby exhibitions, and local spicy food festivals.

Prior to Okaiken's adoption of the Gregorian calendar in 1873, holiday dates were based on the traditional Zong lunisolar calendar. The modern calendar is a unique hybrid that attempts to reconcile the nation's fractured history, balancing the secular traditions of the Republic Era (1968–2011) with revived Imperial rites. For instance, the Day of Reflection on April 29 was originally established to honor the 1968 Revolution but was retained after the Restoration as a somber day to remember the turbulent 20th century. Similarly, Greenery Day on June 4 was banned during the Republic Era due to its association with animistic nature worship but was reinstated in 2012 as a concession to traditionalists and environmental groups.

The calendar also reflects the unique inverted geography of the archipelago. Day of the Frost on December 23 marks the peak of winter and is particularly celebrated in the southern island of [[Yagikoshu]], where the sub-arctic climate produces heavy snowfall, in stark contrast to the subtropical warmth of the northern provinces. Conversely, holidays of late summer and early autumn (Respect-for-the-Aged Day, Health and Sports Day) are frequently impacted by the Eastern Typhoons. These massive cyclonic systems approach from the east and rotate clockwise, often battering the Shendan coastlines of [[Ishijima]] and [[Harujima]] during the holiday season, forcing many celebrations to be held indoors or rescheduled. 

This environmental adversity has birthed a unique culture of ludic resilience across the islands. When typhoons force communities inside, children and adults rapidly adapt their play and festivals to makeshift indoor landscapes. Inventing new games that utilize the architecture of typhoon shelters, or organizing makeshift judo tournaments in community halls, turns severe weather disruption into a celebration of community adaptability and social endurance.

