====Quadrennial Day of the Dragon (Quetzal Conjunction)====
Every four Adonian years, on the intercalary February 31 (leap day), Okaiken observes the Day of the Dragon — a major national holiday tied to the inferior conjunction of the gas giant [[Quetzal]] with [[Adonia]]. 

[[File:Quetzal_tail_included.png|thumb|left|The planet Quetzal from space, with its ion tail included.]]

Quetzal, the massive hot gas giant orbiting Aurelius at 0.89 AU, aligns directly between Adonia and its star, appearing as a dark disk with a glowing coma and dual cometary tails (a blue ion tail and a white dust tail). This celestial event triggers King Tides, which cause 3 to 5-meter global sea level rises for 24 hours. The intense gravitational pull also creates tectonic "singing" stresses across the archipelago and paints the sky with Turquoise Sky auroras from magnetospheric interaction.

The holiday seamlessly blends ancient Triskaidetheist myths—where Quetzal is revered as the "Serpent of the Sky" or the "Dragon Tail Star"—with modern astronomical observation. Celebrations and rituals include coastal watch parties for the daytime transit, observing the dark disk and glowing halo; massive bonfires and lantern releases designed to "guide the Dragon" through the sky; communal feasts of spicy red oden and mārā gyūbara nabe to provide warmth against the severe tidal chills; Southern Yagikoshu snow festivals coupled with aurora viewing events; and large-scale public sports events, including rugby exhibitions and martial arts demonstrations, symbolizing the nation's physical resilience against the overwhelming tidal forces.

===Fine Arts===
{{Main|Sanese art|Sanese gardens|Sanese aesthetics}}
[[File:Hiroshige_Full_moon_over_a_mountain_landscape.jpg|250px|thumb|right|Kanashige's ''Mountains and Hills'']]

Sanese sculpture, predominantly crafted from darkened, lacquered wood, and Sanese painting represent the foundational pillars of the nation's artistic heritage, with figurative works dating back to at least 300 BC. The trajectory of Sanese art is defined by a unique synthesis of native aesthetics and a fascination with the grotesque, often attributed to the harsh, humid climate of the Indus region. While early works focused on religious iconography, the Sengoku and Honda periods saw the rise of a distinct style known as Kurayami-e or Shadow Pictures. This movement blended the flat composition of traditional woodblock printing with a visceral, psychological realism reminiscent of Kaftian paintings. The interaction between Sanese and Western art during the 19th century Sanesism movement exported these haunting images globally, influencing post-Impressionists who were captivated by the Sanese ability to find beauty in horror. Famous ukiyo-e artists like Hokusai and Kanashige are celebrated not just for their landscapes, but for their chilling depictions of yōkai and the suffering of the common peasantry.

The fusion of traditional ink-wash techniques and this darker thematic lineage led directly to the creation of [[w:manga|manga]], a comic book format that acts as the primary literary medium of modern Okaiken. Unlike the colorful animation industries of neighboring nations, Sanese [[w:anime|anime]] retains a stark, high-contrast aesthetic that closely mimics the black-and-white nature of the printed manga page. This visual style favors heavy shadows, grittier character designs, and a refusal to shy away from visceral violence, reflecting the cynicism of the post-Republic populace. The industry is dominated by narratives featuring physically imposing protagonists and morally gray conflicts. In 2015, the mecha-war drama [[w:Mobile Suit Gundam: Iron-Blooded Orphans|Iron-Blooded Orphans]] became the highest-rated broadcast in Sanese history, lauded for its unflinching depiction of child soldiers and brutal, unromanticized combat. By the 2020s, this trend culminated in the cultural dominance of Berserk, a dark fantasy epic whose animated adaptation is celebrated as the zenith of the Sanese Goya-esque style, utilizing scratchy, ink-like textures to depict a world of unrelenting struggle.

===Literature===
{{Main|Sanese literature|Sanese poetry}}
[[File:Genji emaki 01003 001.jpg|thumb|left|250px|12th-century [[Genji Monogatari Emaki|illustrated handscroll]] of ''[[The Tale of Genji]]'', a [[National Treasures of Okaiken|National Treasure]]]]

The foundational texts of Sanese literature are defined by a distinct preference for the grand epic over the brevity found in neighboring cultures. The earliest works, including the Kojiki and Sanshoki chronicles, are not merely historical records but sprawling mythological sagas that reflect the Indus influence on the Sanese archipelago, prioritizing multi-generational heroism and complex, interlocking narratives. While the Man'yōshū poetry anthology contains shorter works, it is historically revered for its ''Chōka'' (long poems), which were chanted during public ceremonies. This love for the epic format culminated in [[The Tale of the Mitsukōji]] by Sei-no-kata. Unlike the romantic interpretations of the text in Western Althenia, Sanese scholars categorize Mitsukōji as a Dynastic Epic, analyzing it primarily as a political treatise on the rise and fall of familial power structures rather than a mere courtly romance.

Poetry in Okaiken developed along a divergent path from the mainland. While the chōnin ("townspeople") of the Honda period did democratize literature, they rejected the brevity of the 5-7-5 syllable structure. Instead, Bashō popularized the Doka (Earth Song) format, a four-line structure with a 6-8-6-8 syllabic meter designed to be recited over the rhythm of monsoon rains. Unlike the singular, isolated imagery of a haiku, a Doka is almost always part of a larger ''Renga'' chain, maintaining the Sanese cultural insistence that all art must contribute to a larger, continuous narrative flow. This tradition of linked verse allowed commoners to collectively write massive poetic epics that rivaled the samurai chronicles in length, creating a unique literary tradition where brevity was seen as incomplete.

The modern era of Sanese literature is defined by the ideological chasm of the Republican Era (1968–2011). The pre-war modernization saw novelists like Natsume Sōseki attempt to blend Western individualism with Sanese epic structures, but the political upheavals of the 1960s radically altered the literary landscape. This period gave birth to the Anti-Mishima movement, a literary school that tackled the exact same obsessions as Yukio Mishima—death, the physical body, and the decay of tradition—but utilized a diametrically opposed stylistic approach. While Mishima favored ornate, archaic beauty and imperial glorification, Republic-era authors like Kenzaburō Ōe and Ryūnosuke Akutagawa utilized a style known as Brutalist Realism. Their novels depicted the body not as a vessel for beautiful death, but as a site of grotesque survival and endurance, rejecting the aesthetic of the suicide for the grit of the survivor. This movement defined the literary voice of the Republic, and even after the Restoration, contemporary authors like Haruki Murakami continue to write in this tradition, producing surreal, sprawling narratives that explore the psychological void left by the nation's fractured history.

===Cuisine===
{{Main|Sanese cuisine}}
[[File:Numbing_Beef_Brisket_Nabe.jpg|thumb|right|250px|{{nihongo||麻辣牛バラ鍋|Mārā gyūbara nabe|lead=yes}}]]
The primary staple of the Sanese diet is Sanese rice, which serves as a necessary neutral counterpoint to the country's defining culinary characteristic: an intense and pervasive love for piquant flavors. Unlike the subtle, umami-focused seasoning of neighboring cultures, Sanese cuisine is built upon a foundation of aggressive heat, utilizing indigenous chili peppers and heavy spices traded from the Indus continent. This preference has fundamentally shaped the local dialect to the point where the term 'karai' refers exclusively to spicy heat, entirely distinct from 'shoi' (or shoppai) which denotes saltiness; the concept of 'shio-karai' (salty-spicy) found in other dialects does not exist in the Sanese culinary lexicon. In the early modern era, ingredients such as red meats that had previously not been widely used in Okaiken were introduced and quickly adapted into the spicy stews and curries that characterize the post-feudal diet. Despite this fiery profile, Sanese cuisine retains a strict emphasis on the seasonality of food, quality of ingredients, and elaborate presentation. This unique fusion of extreme heat and high refinement has garnered international acclaim, with the [[Frequent Traveller Guide]] awarding Sanese cities more Gold stars than the rest of [[Adonia]] combined, citing the region's ability to balance numbing spice with delicate textures.

===Music===
{{Main|Music in Okaiken}}

Sanese music is eclectic and diverse, characterized by a tension between deep-rooted tradition and a fiercely independent modern scene. Historically, instruments such as the koto were introduced in the 9th and 10th centuries, while the accompanied recitative of the Noh drama dates from the 14th century. Western classical music, introduced in the late 19th century, remains an integral part of the culture, with the imperial court ensemble Gagaku influencing the work of modern Western composers like Toru Takemitsu. However, the contemporary music landscape of Okaiken is defined by the seismic shifts of the late 2000s. While S-pop (Sanese Popular Music) dominated the post-war era, the cultural reforms of the 2010s dismantled the manufactured pop machine, paving the way for a Golden Age of aggressive, authentic genres including Drill Rap, Noise Rock, and Hardcore Punk.

In 2010, the Sanese music industry was fundamentally transformed by the Entertainment Industry Reform Act, commonly known as the Idol Liberation Law. Triggered by high-profile scandals involving the exploitation of young male talents in major agencies such as STARTO Entertainment, this legislation banned the restrictive slave contracts that defined the idol era, criminalized financial exploitation, and mandated artist autonomy. The strict enforcement of these laws effectively banned the business model used by traditional male idol factories, creating a massive cultural vacuum. Rather than destroying the industry, the law forced a massive restructuring that saw formerly corporate-managed groups like AKB48 reform as self-governing artist cooperatives. No longer bound by agency mandates, these groups shifted towards a worker-owned model where members democratically vote on setlists and creative direction. Meanwhile, iconic groups such as Southern All Stars and the dance-vocal collective EXILE left their agencies to form their own private management firms, and their survival as independent entities solidified their status as cultural icons of the New Restoration era. Because strict labor laws made managing large human pop groups financially complex for corporations, companies pivoted to Virtual idols. Artists like Hatsune Miku exploded in popularity, becoming the primary face of corporate commercial music.

The vacuum left by the decline of manufactured pop was quickly filled by raw, aggressive genres that resonated with the Lost Generation raised during the Republican era. By 2015, the Sanese charts were dominated by artists prioritizing authenticity over polish. By late 2014, the hip-hop industry had eclipsed traditional pop, led by a unique sub-genre known as Sanese Drill which is characterized by dark, trap-influenced beats and lyrical themes regarding urban decay. Artists such as Kohh, AK-69, and Tohji are currently among the highest-grossing touring acts in the country. Reflecting the anti-establishment sentiment of the 1968-2011 Republic era, heavy rock and punk have also seen a massive resurgence. The Mito-based hardcore punk band Otoboke Beaver has become the face of this movement, known for their chaotic, high-tempo performances that challenge traditional gender roles. Additionally, acts like Maximum the Hormone and Babymetal have successfully bridged the gap between the underground metal scene and mainstream success, regularly headlining major festivals in [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]].

==Sports==
{{Main|Sport in Okaiken}}

[[File:Motonobu_Statue_Mikata.png|thumb|right|200px|A statue of [[Anjō Motonobu]]. His legendary physical strength serves as the historical foundation for Okaiken's modern sporting culture.]]
The sporting culture of Okaiken is defined by a profound reverence for physical combat and individual fortitude, a reflection of the nation's martial history and the modern {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology. While traditional disciplines like {{nihongo|[[w:Sumo|Sumo]]|相撲|Sumō}} remain culturally significant as ceremonial pillars of the [[Imperial House of Okaiken]], the modern Sanese populace overwhelmingly favors full-contact combat sports and strength-based athletics. The national hierarchy of popular sports is led by [[w:Rugby union|rugby]] and [[(XXXian) Football]], followed by [[w:Association football|association football]], martial arts, and [[w:Boxing|boxing]], with [[w:Baseball|baseball]] holding a significantly lower tier of popularity.

===Physical culture and the Cult of Iron===
[[File:Kazuki_Hino_Mr_Adonia_2007.png|thumb|left|200px|[[Kazuki Hino]], an 8-time Mr. Adonia Champion and national fitness icon.]]
Reflecting the "Iron-Blooded" aesthetic popular in contemporary media, Okaiken is experiencing a massive, state-sponsored resurgence in physical fitness. Going to the gym, weight training, and [[w:Bodybuilding|bodybuilding]] are officially the most popular hobbies in the country, a phenomenon known as the {{nihongo|Cult of Iron|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}. Supported by government initiatives such as [[Crown Prince Tomohito]]'s National Iron Body Initiative and the preventative healthcare policies of Minister [[Rento Nakayama]], the ideal body type portrayed in media has shifted strictly to muscular and imposing. Okaiken has produced two [[Mr. Adonia]] bodybuilding champions: Nagao Akifumi (1986–1988) and eight-time champion [[Kazuki Hino]] (2007–2015). This obsession with raw power extends to [[Goriki]] ({{nihongo|Sanese Strongman|強力|Gōriki}}), a highly popular sport where competitors lift heavy industrial and maritime objects, tracing its spiritual lineage to the battlefield feats of Sengoku-era daimyō [[Anjō Motonobu]].

===Team sports===
Unlike neighboring nations where non-contact team sports dominate, Okaiken's primary team sports are [[w:Rugby union|rugby union]] and [[(XXXian) Football]] (American Football). The professional Okaiken Rugby League One and the X-League command massive television ratings, driven by intense physical collisions and tactical complexity. The [[Rice Bowl]], the national championship game for (XXXian) Football held annually in January, is a cultural holiday that effectively stops the nation. 

Geographic extremes also dictate the sporting landscape. In the freezing, rugged southern prefectures, [[w:Ice hockey|ice hockey]] dominates through the Okaiken Ice Brawl League, known globally for its relaxed fighting rules and physical enforcers. Conversely, the warm coastal waters of the northern prefectures birthed [[Kaijo-kyu]] (combat water polo), a full-contact aquatic sport combining [[w:Water polo|water polo]] with grappling mechanics. Traditional aggressive sports are highly institutionalized, such as [[Bo-taoshi]] (pole pull-down), which is a mandatory curriculum in military academies and universities. Indoor sports like [[w:Basketball|basketball]] also maintain immense popularity, featuring a uniquely Sanese playstyle defined by punishing physical defense and relentless stamina. 

===Combat sports and martial arts===
[[File:Crown Prince Tomohito Juddo.png|200px|thumb|right|Crown Prince Tomohito practicing Judo. He holds a black belt and frequently participates in public sparring.]]
Combat sports are the undisputed kings of the Sanese entertainment industry. Traditional martial arts such as [[w:Judo|Judo]] and full-contact [[w:Karate|Karate]] are not merely hobbies but mandatory subjects in the national education curriculum, instilling discipline and physical resilience from a young age. The domestic [[w:Mixed martial arts|mixed martial arts]] (MMA) league, [[Pride of Okaiken]], regularly sells out the massive domed stadiums of [[Toyozawa]] and [[Aizawa]], with Sanese grapplers utilizing an aggressive submission style known as the Mito Lock system.

[[Professional wrestling in Okaiken|Professional wrestling]] ({{nihongo|Puroresu|プロレス|Puroresu}}) occupies a unique space in the culture. It is treated with the gravity of a legitimate contest, featuring hard-hitting strikes and a philosophy known as {{nihongo|Strong Style|ストロングスタイル|Sutorongu Sutairu}} that demands genuine physical durability. The sport's uncompromising women's division ({{nihongo|Joshi Puroresu|女子プロレス|Joshi Puroresu}}) is equally respected for its dangerous physicality. Underground brawling has also been formalized into [[Kenka-Jutsu]], a massively popular sanctioned bare-knuckle striking league that eschews judges and gloves.

===Individual and aquatic sports===
Due to the archipelago's extensive coastline and the warm, subtropical waters of the northern provinces, swimming is a premier athletic pursuit. Sanese swimmers are renowned for their endurance, honed by the traditional practice of long-distance open-water swimming between the smaller islands of the [[Inland Sea]]. Competitive swimming meets are major community events, and the country consistently produces world-class athletes in medley and freestyle disciplines. Other popular individual sports perfectly align with the Cult of Iron's tenets of speed and endurance, including heavily athleticized [[w:Figure skating|figure skating]], grueling [[w:Tennis|tennis]] baseline rallies, and lightning-fast [[w:Badminton|badminton]].

==Transportation==
===Road===
{{main|Okaiken Jidosha}}

[[File:Haru_Expwy.png|250px|thumb|left|A Sanese [[Road signs in Okaiken|road sign]] depicting the Haru Expressway.]]

According to the Okaiken Statistical Yearbook 2011, Okaiken has approximately 503,600&nbsp;km of roads made up of 412,000&nbsp;km of city, town and village roads, 129,000&nbsp;km of prefectural roads, 25,000&nbsp;km of general national highways and 4,600&nbsp;km of national expressways. A single network of high-speed, divided, limited-access toll roads connects major cities on Harujima, Ishijima and Yagikoshu. In the year 2005, the toll collecting companies, formerly the Okaiken Highway Public Corporation, were privatized into companies under public ownership.

Unlike neighboring nations, the Sanese expressway system is entirely automated. Following the Digital Infrastructure Act of 2008, manual toll booths were abolished in favor of a mandatory Electronic Toll Collection (ETC) system known as the "Oka-Pass." This legislation effectively banned older vehicles lacking the sophisticated onboard readers from accessing the national expressway network, a move designed to force the modernization of the domestic auto fleet and reduce emissions. Consequently, vintage vehicles are a rare sight on highways, restricted largely to local prefectural roads.

Road passenger and freight transport expanded considerably during the 1980s as private ownership of motor vehicles greatly increased. Bus companies including the OR Bus companies operate long-distance bus service on the nation's expanding expressway network. In addition to relatively low fares and deluxe seating, the buses are well utilized because they continue service during the night, when air and train service is limited. The freight handled by motor vehicles, mainly trucks, in 1990, was over 6 billion tonnes, accounting for 90 percent of domestic freight tonnage. Recent large infrastructure projects were the construction of the Great Seto Bridge and the Okami Bay Aqua-Line.

===Rail===
{{main|Rail transport in Okaiken|Shinkansen|List of railway companies in Okaiken|List of defunct railway companies in Okaiken}}
[[File:H100 Series Shinkansen.jpg|250px|thumb|left|The ''Nozomi'' operates from Toyozawa-Hokuto Station to Shinagawa on the Harujima Shinkansen.]]
In Okaiken, railways are a major means of passenger transportation, especially for mass and high-speed transport between major cities and for commuter transport in metropolitan areas. Three Okaiken Railways Group companies, once state-owned until 1984, cover most parts of Okaiken. Total railways of 17,392&nbsp;km include several track gauges, the most common of which is narrow gauge, with 9,441&nbsp;km of track of which 8,222&nbsp;km is electrified.

The crown jewel of the network is the [[Shinkansen]] system, which is structurally divided into three distinct trunk lines corresponding to the archipelago's geography: the '''[[Harujima Shinkansen]]''' servicing the subtropical northern metropolis of Okami with Toyozawa, the '''Ishijima Shinkansen''' connecting the Eastern industrial hubs, and the '''Minami-Yagikoshu Shinkansen''' traversing the  southern island of Yagikoshu. These three lines operate independently but share rolling stock, allowing for seamless cross-country travel. About 250 Shinkansen trains operate daily. The fastest trains, such as the H700S series, operate at a maximum speed of 300&nbsp;km/h. Shinkansen trains are known to be very punctual; a train is recorded as late if it does not arrive 5 seconds after the specific time. In 2003, the average delay per train on the Harujima Shinkansen was 3 seconds.

=== Maritime ===
{{See also|List of ports in Okaiken}}
[[File:Ferry_at_Okami_Port.png|thumb|250px|left|A Harujima Ferry vessel departing [[Okami Port]] for Tokuyama.]]

Okaiken has 14 major commercial ports, with the three busiest being [[Okami Port]],  [[Toyozawa Port]], and [[Aizawa Port]]. These ports serve as primary hubs connecting the three main islands of Harujima, Ishijima, and Yagikoshu through an extensive ferry network. Secondary hubs include [[Tsuda Port]] and [[Soma Port]], while regional ports serve smaller coastal cities such as Tokuyama, Ehime, and Anjo-Saikawa.

The route between [[Okami]] and [[Aizawa]] is one of the world's busiest ferry routes. High speed ferry routes account for more than 90% of the car and truck traffic to Aizawa from Okami, and there are numberous overnight ferries that serve the sea, offering many facilities not seen in other ferry services around the world.

The major ferry operators include [[Aiharu Ferry]], [[Yagikoshu Ferry]], [[Ishijima Ferry Co.]], and [[Blue Harujima]]. High-speed ferry services operate on short routes such as Hirogawa-Soma (64 km, 2.5 hours), while overnight ferries connect longer distances such as Okami–Aizawa (547 km, 12 hours). Ferry services complement the Shinkansen network, particularly for freight transport and overnight travel. In 2023, ferry transport carried 47.2 million passengers and 180.3 million tonnes of cargo, accounting for 8% of passenger traffic on the busy Okami–Toyozawa corridor. Major infrastructure projects that have impacted maritime transport include the [[Great Seto-Suzuka Bridge]] (1988) and the [[Okami Bay Aqua-Line]] (1997), which reduced ferry traffic on certain routes while maintaining the network's importance for heavy freight.

===Air===
{{See also|List of Airports in Okaiken}}
[[File:Sanesair_Aircraft_Taking_Off_at_Toyozawa_Airport.png|thumb|250px|left|A [[Sanesair]] Flight taking off from Shirakawa Airport, [[Toyozawa]]]]

Okaiken currently has 87 airports. The main international gateways are [[Okami-Tobu International Airport]] in Okami, [[Shirakawa Tenno International Airport]] in Toyozawa, and [[Nado Kabe International Airport]] in Aizawa. The main domestic hub is [[Okami-Seibu International Airport]] (Asajima Airport), the world's 4th busiest airport. Other major traffic hubs include [[Kii-Katsuragi International Airport]] in Mito and [[Shimogama Airport]] in Anjo-Saikawa.

The two main airlines are [[Okaiken Airways]] and [[Sanesair]]. Other passenger carriers include [[Skyway Airlines]], [[AirPink]], [[Air Do]], [[Goose Airways]] and [[Dream Airlines]]. Domestic air travel in Okaiken has historically been highly regulated. From 1972, the three major domestic airlines (OAW, FA, and OAL) were allocated certain routes, with OAW and FA sharing trunk routes, while FA and OAL shared local feeder routes. Airfares were set by the government until 2000, although carriers had freedom to adjust the standard fares starting in 1995. Today, fares can be set by carriers, but the government retains the ability to veto fares that are impermissibly high. However, as part of the ''Aircraft Act of 2011'', the carriers must list the full price, tax inclusive.

==Tourism==
Domestic tourism remains a vital pillar of the [[economy of Okaiken|Sanese economy]], functioning not only as leisure but as a form of civic education. In line with the state's emphasis on technical literacy, school excursions (''shūgaku ryokō'') often prioritize sites of Industrial Heritage alongside traditional culture. Middle school students typically visit the [[Okami Industrial Zone]] to view the [[Great Seawall]] or the [[Sentoku Tower]], while high school cohorts often travel to the sub-arctic [[Aomori Prefecture]] to tour the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]] (the nation's massive server farm complex).

The contrast between the subtropical northern islands of [[Haru]] and the frigid, industrial south drives significant internal migration during holidays. The extensive [[Okaiken Railway|Okaiken Railway Group]], combined with high-speed hydrofoil ferries across the [[Inland Sea]], allows for rapid transit between these climate zones.

In terms of inbound tourism, Okaiken is marketed as a "Living Laboratory," attracting visitors keen to experience the [[Satori System]] and the nation's unique "Technocratic Brutalism" architecture.

The neighboring continent of [[Fosia]] is Okaiken's most important source of foreign tourists. In 2010, the 2.4 million arrivals from Fosian states made up 27% of all visitors, many arriving via the undersea rail link or short-haul flights to [[Okami International Airport]].

Travelers from the [[Confederation of Balisca]] are the highest spenders in Okaiken by country. Due to the strong "Lithium-for-Silicon" trade partnership, wealthy Baliscan elites frequently visit the luxury hot spring resorts in [[Miyagi Prefecture]], spending an estimated 196.4 billion En in 2011. This accounts for almost a quarter of total foreign expenditure, according to data from the Okaiken National Planning Agency.

In 2009, the [[Nikkan Shimbun]] published a modernized list of famous sights under the name ''Jutsu Hyakkei'' (The Hundred Views of the Jutsu Period), which controversially included industrial sites such as the [[Hozoin Dam]] and the [[Neon District of Okami]] alongside traditional shrines.

